---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
const desc = 'Free LLM inference throughput calculator: tokens per second required from concurrent users, accelerators needed, and the latency each user sees at your batch size.';
const jsonld = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'WebApplication',
  name: 'Inference Throughput Calculator', applicationCategory: 'DeveloperApplication',
  operatingSystem: 'Web', offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
  url: 'https://theworldofai.org/calculators/inference-throughput/',
});

const faqs = [
  { q: 'How many GPUs do I need to serve 500 concurrent users?',
    a: 'It depends entirely on response length and latency target, not on the user count alone. At 400 output tokens delivered in 8 seconds, 500 concurrent requests demand 25,000 tokens a second from the fleet. At 2,500 tokens a second per accelerator and 70 percent usable utilisation, that is 15 accelerators.' },
  { q: 'Why does batching raise throughput but hurt latency?',
    a: 'Decode is bound by memory bandwidth: generating one token requires reading the model weights regardless of how many requests are in flight. Batching amortises that single read across many requests, so total tokens a second rises sharply. Each individual request waits for the batch to form and to step together, so its own time to completion gets worse.' },
  { q: 'Should I size on average load or peak load?',
    a: 'Peak concurrency. A fleet sized to the mean queues through every busy hour, and queueing delay compounds because slower responses hold sessions open longer, which raises concurrency further. Size on peak, then decide separately whether to autoscale down off-peak.' },
  { q: 'Why is usable utilisation set below 100 percent?',
    a: 'Because a real fleet never converts every accelerator-second into output tokens. Scheduling gaps between batches, model loading after a restart, cancelled and failed requests, health checks and rolling deploys all consume capacity. Seventy percent is a reasonable planning figure; measure your own before committing.' },
  { q: 'Where do I get a per-accelerator throughput number?',
    a: 'From your own benchmark, on your own model, at your own batch size and sequence length, on your own serving stack. It is not a property of the hardware: quantization, attention implementation, batch policy and context length all move it, often by more than a hardware generation does.' },
];
---
<Base title="LLM Inference Throughput Calculator: tokens per second and GPUs needed — The World of AI" description={desc} current="/calculators/" jsonld={jsonld}>
  <h1>Inference Throughput Calculator</h1>
  <p class="answer">Serving capacity is set by how many tokens a second the fleet must produce and how many each accelerator can produce. Divide the first by the second and you have the accelerator count; the answer moves sharply with batch size, because batching raises throughput per GPU while raising latency for each individual request.</p>
  <p class="meta-line">Formula and defaults last reviewed: 2026-08-31.</p>

  <div class="calc">
    <label>Peak concurrent requests <input type="number" id="users" value="500" step="10" min="1" /></label>
    <label>Output tokens per response <input type="number" id="out" value="400" step="50" min="1" /></label>
    <label>Target time to complete a response (seconds) <input type="number" id="resp" value="8" step="1" min="1" /></label>
    <label>Throughput per accelerator (tokens/sec, batched) <input type="number" id="pergpu" value="2500" step="100" min="1" /></label>
    <label>Usable utilisation (%) <input type="number" id="util" value="70" step="5" min="1" /></label>
    <div class="out" id="out2" aria-live="polite"></div>
  </div>

  <h2>How the math works</h2>
  <p>Required throughput = concurrent requests × output tokens ÷ target response time.
    Accelerators = required throughput ÷ (per-accelerator throughput × usable utilisation).
    Utilisation below 100% covers scheduling gaps, model loading, failed requests and the
    reality that a fleet sized to exactly its peak has no margin at all.</p>
  <h2>Prefill and decode are different problems</h2>
  <p>Generation has two phases with different characteristics. Prefill processes the whole
    prompt at once and is compute-bound; decode produces one token at a time and is bound by
    memory bandwidth, because every token requires reading the model weights and the KV cache.
    That is why decode throughput, the number this calculator asks for, is usually the binding
    constraint, and why long prompts and long outputs load a fleet quite differently.</p>
  <h2>What this does not tell you</h2>
  <p>Per-accelerator throughput is not a property of the hardware alone: it depends on the model,
    quantization, batch size, sequence length, and the serving stack. Take the figure from your
    own benchmark on your own model rather than a vendor sheet. Memory is a separate limit that
    can bind before throughput does, which the
    <a href="/calculators/vram/">VRAM estimator</a> covers.</p>

  <h2>What does the default fleet look like?</h2>
  <p>Five hundred concurrent requests, 400 output tokens each, an 8 second target, 2,500 tokens a
    second per accelerator, 70 percent usable.</p>
  <p>Required throughput is 500 × 400 ÷ 8, which is 25,000 tokens a second. Effective throughput
    per accelerator is 2,500 × 0.70, or 1,750. Twenty-five thousand divided by 1,750 is 14.3, so
    15 accelerators. Each user sees 400 ÷ 8, or 50 tokens a second, roughly 37 words a second,
    which is comfortably faster than reading speed.</p>
  <p>Relaxing the latency target to 16 seconds halves the fleet to 8 accelerators for the same
    number of users. That single tradeoff, perceived speed against hardware, is usually the
    largest cost lever in a serving budget.</p>

  <h2>Does streaming change what latency target I need?</h2>
  <p>It changes which number the user experiences. Without streaming they wait for the whole
    response, so time to completion is the metric. With streaming they see the first token quickly
    and then read along, so the two figures that matter are time to first token, set by prefill
    over the prompt, and the per-token rate during decode.</p>
  <p>Anything above about 20 tokens a second exceeds comfortable reading speed, so a streaming
    interface can often accept a longer total completion time than a blocking one without feeling
    slower. Sizing against time to completion when the product streams overbuys hardware; sizing
    against the per-token rate when it does not underbuys it.</p>

  <Faq items={faqs} />

  <div class="cite-box">Cite this tool: <code>"Inference Throughput Calculator." The World of AI,
    theworldofai.org/calculators/inference-throughput/.</code></div>

  <script is:inline>
  (function () {
    function n(i) { return parseFloat(document.getElementById(i).value) || 0; }
    function f(v, d) { return v.toLocaleString(undefined, { maximumFractionDigits: d === undefined ? 0 : d }); }
    var IDS = ['users','out','resp','pergpu','util'];
    function calc() {
      var need = n('resp') > 0 ? (n('users') * n('out')) / n('resp') : 0;
      var eff = n('pergpu') * (n('util') / 100);
      var gpus = eff > 0 ? Math.ceil(need / eff) : 0;
      var perUser = n('out') > 0 && n('resp') > 0 ? n('out') / n('resp') : 0;
      document.getElementById('out2').innerHTML =
        'Fleet throughput required: <b>' + f(need) + ' tokens/sec</b><br>' +
        'Accelerators at ' + f(n('util')) + '% usable: <b>' + gpus + '</b><br>' +
        'Each user sees about <b>' + f(perUser, 1) + ' tokens/sec</b>, which is roughly ' + f(perUser * 0.75, 1) + ' words a second<br>' +
        '<span class="sub">Headroom matters more than the mean: size on peak concurrency, not average, or every busy hour queues.</span>';
    }
    IDS.forEach(function (i) {
      document.getElementById(i).addEventListener('input', calc);
      document.getElementById(i).addEventListener('change', calc);
    });
    calc();
  })();
  </script>
  <style>
    .calc { border: 1px solid #e6e4de; border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px 20px; background: #fafaf6; margin: 1.4em 0; }
    .calc label { display: block; margin: 0 0 12px; font-size: 14px; color: #26262b; }
    .calc input, .calc select { display: block; margin-top: 4px; padding: 7px 9px; font-size: 15px;
      border: 1px solid #cfcdc6; border-radius: 6px; width: 220px; max-width: 100%; }
    .out { margin-top: 14px; padding-top: 14px; border-top: 1px solid #e6e4de; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.7; }
    .out b { color: #201868; }
    .sub { color: #7A8A9E; font-size: 13.5px; }
  </style>
</Base>
